  "analysis": "The task is to maximize total points by repeatedly erasing a contiguous substring consisting of identical digits (0s or 1s). The points for erasing a block depend only on its length via array a[1..n], where n ≤ 100. After erasing, the remaining parts concatenate, so blocks of the same digit can merge and yield larger future erasures. This is a classic interval DP problem similar to the Remove Boxes problem, where merging blocks increases the potential length of the next removal. The optimal solution can be obtained with a top-down memoized DP over intervals [l, r] with an extra parameter k representing additional characters of the same color as s[r] that are attached to the right end of the interval. The transition either removes the last block now (length k+1, giving a[k+1] points) or merges the last block with a previous occurrence of the same character inside [l, r-1]. The recurrence is well-known for this kind of problem and runs in O(n^3) states with manageable memory for n ≤ 100.\n\nPlan:\n- Implement a C++17 solution using a top-down DP function solve(l, r, k) returning the maximum points for s[l..r] with k extra trailing characters equal to s[r].\n- Use memoization arrays: dp[l][r][k] and a visited flag to avoid recomputation. Base case: if l > r return 0. For l == r, the result is a[k+1].\n- Recurrence:\n  1) Remove the last block now: solve(l, r-1, 0) + a[k+1].\n  2) For each i in [l, r-1] with s[i] == s[r], merge blocks by removing (i+1..r-1) first: solve(l, i, k+1) + solve(i+1, r-1, 0).\n  Take the maximum over these options.\n- Read input, run solve(0, n-1, 0) and print the result as a long long.\n- Provide shell commands to write the code, compile, and test with the given samples.\n\nPotential edge cases:\n- All characters equal: many merge opportunities; algorithm handles via k parameter.\n- n = 1: trivial, answer = a[1].\n- Large a[i] values up to 1e9; use long long.\n\nI will provide a complete solution in C++17 along with batch commands to create the file, compile, and test with samples.",
  "plan": "Next steps:\n1) Write a C++17 program implementing the described DP (top-down with memoization).\n2) Compile the program.\n3) Run the provided sample tests to verify correctness.\n4) If needed, run additional random tests to validate edge cases.",
